{"name":"November Skies","hex":"#7cafb9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"november","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of November of the ICAO/NATO radiotelephony alphabet."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of November from the NATO/ICAO Phonetic Alphabet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/november"},{"term":"skies","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of sky though essentially synonymous with singular form; the heavens; the firmament; the atmosphere.","plural of skie."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of sky"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/skies"}],"entries":[{"title":"November","blocks":[{"text":"November is the 11th and penultimate month of the year in the Julian and Gregorian calendars. Its length is 30 days. November was the ninth month of the calendar of Romulus c. 750 BC. November retained its name when January and February were added to the Roman calendar.\nNovember is a month of late spring in the Southern Hemisphere and late autumn in the Northern Hemisphere. Therefore, November in the Southern Hemisphere is the seasonal equivalent of May in the Northern Hemisphere and vice versa. In Ancient Rome, Ludi Plebeii was held from November 4–17, Epulum Jovis was held on November 13 and Brumalia celebrations began on November 24. These dates do not correspond to the modern Gregorian calendar."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/November","wikidata":"Q125"},{"title":"Sky","blocks":[{"text":"The sky is an unobstructed view upward from the surface of the Earth. It includes the atmosphere and outer space. It may also be considered a place between the ground and outer space, thus distinct from outer space."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/July_night_sky_%2835972569256%29.jpg/330px-July_night_sky_%2835972569256%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedi